 gives an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=4,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $4$ and $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/835b4206e04d064485d02635cfd4e2ad39a934b1d2b38296cc6463a265f5d931.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/cd8e0a10d162d30be895fa4ce49e3944e4e1a3ee9f79aa2fafe70c0edd2c0d61.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-122829/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122829"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
